ControlMaster=auto', '-o', 'ControlPersist=15m', '-o', 'ControlPath=\"/storage/home/sue217/.cache/datalad/sockets/f7ed4923\"', 'username@c-alias']. Exit code=255\n| stdout: None\n| stderr: None\n```\n\nI noticed that the host name was incorrectly de-capitalized so I added this host name to the list\n```\nHost C, C-Alias, c-alias\n    User username\n    ForwardAgent yes\n    ProxyCommand ssh -q B-alias nc C 22\n    ServerAliveInterval 240\n    ServerAliveCountMax 2\n```\n\nAfter this modification \n`datalad install dataset`\n\nWorks like a charm! \n\nOnly issue is that `git annex sync` doesn't work unless you remove the lowercase host entry. No idea why on this one 🤷🏻‍♂️\n\n#### What version of DataLad are you using (run `datalad --version`)? On what operating system (consider running `datalad wtf`)?\ndatalad 0.11.0\n#### Is there anything else that would be useful to know in this context?\nI changed the user/host names in the output/example files for privacy.\n#### Have you had any success using DataLad before? (to assess your expertise/prior luck.  We would welcome your testimonial additions to https://github.com/datalad/datalad/wiki/Testimonials as well)\ndatalad is great!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1547610863,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NDY0MjkwMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3123#issuecomment-454642903"},"message":"\u003e #### What is the problem?\n\u003e \n\u003e Candidate source URL is invalid and does not match ssh config host name, preventing `datalad install` from running correctly.\n\u003e #### What steps will reproduce the problem?\n\u003e \n\u003e I am attempting to install across SSH hops.. so my config file looks something like\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Host A.edu\n\u003e      User seldamat\n\u003e      ForwardAgent yes\n\u003e      ServerAliveInterval 240\n\u003e      ServerAliveCountMax 2\n\u003e      IdentityFile ~/.ssh/rsa\n\u003e \n\u003e Host B, B-Alias\n\u003e     User seldamat\n\u003e     ForwardAgent yes\n\u003e     ProxyCommand ssh -q A nc  B 22\n\u003e     ServerAliveInterval 240\n\u003e     ServerAliveCountMax 2\n\u003e     IdentityFile ~/.ssh/rsa\n\u003e \n\u003e Host C, C-Alias\n\u003e     User username\n\u003e     ForwardAgent yes\n\u003e     ProxyCommand ssh -q B-alias nc C 22\n\u003e     ServerAliveInterval 240\n\u003e     ServerAliveCountMax 2\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e My .gitmodule looks like\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e [submodule \"dataset\"]\n\u003e \tpath = dataset\n\u003e \turl = ssh://admin@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e I am running (note that this is being run within a dataset itself)\n\u003e `datalad install dataset`\n\u003e \n\u003e Error message is (excerpt):\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e [INFO   ] Cloning ssh://username@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git [1 other candidates] into '/gpfs/dataset'\n\u003e ssh_exchange_identification: Connection closed by remote host\n\u003e [WARNING] Failed to run cmd ['ssh', '-fN', '-o', 'ControlMaster=auto', '-o', 'ControlPersist=15m', '-o', 'ControlPath=\"/storage/home/sue217/.cache/datalad/sockets/f7ed4923\"', 'username@c-alias']. Exit code=255\n\u003e | stdout: None\n\u003e | stderr: None\n\u003e ssh_exchange_identification: Connection closed by remote host\n\u003e [WARNING] Failed to run cmd ['ssh', '-fN', '-o', 'ControlMaster=auto', '-o', 'ControlPersist=15m', '-o', 'ControlPath=\"/storage/home/sue217/.cache/datalad/sockets/f7ed4923\"', 'username@c-alias']. Exit code=255\n\u003e | stdout: None\n\u003e | stderr: None\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e I noticed that the host name was incorrectly de-capitalized so I added this host name to the list\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e Host C, C-Alias, c-alias\n\u003e     User username\n\u003e     ForwardAgent yes\n\u003e     ProxyCommand ssh -q B-alias nc C 22\n\u003e     ServerAliveInterval 240\n\u003e     ServerAliveCountMax 2\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e After this modification\n\u003e `datalad install dataset`\n\u003e \n\u003e Works like a charm!\n\u003e \n\u003e Only issue is that `git annex sync` doesn't work unless you remove the lowercase host entry. No idea why on this one 🤷🏻‍♂️\n\u003e #### What version of DataLad are you using (run `datalad --version`)? On what operating system (consider running `datalad wtf`)?\n\u003e \n\u003e datalad 0.11.0\n\u003e #### Is there anything else that would be useful to know in this context?\n\u003e \n\u003e I changed the user/host names in the output/example files for privacy.\n\u003e #### Have you had any success using DataLad before? (to assess your expertise/prior luck. We would welcome your testimonial additions to https://github.com/datalad/datalad/wiki/Testimonials as well)\n\u003e \n\u003e datalad is great!\n\nthanks - really glad to hear\n\nI feel that somewhere we or python library we use forces hostnames to lower case since generally hostnames should be not case sensitive, see e.g. https://serverfault.com/questions/261341/is-the-hostname-case-sensitive for additional pointers:\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import RI; print(RI(\"ssh://username@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git\").hostname)'\nc-alias\n```\nbut interestingly it is a \"side-effect\" of guessing the RI (resource identifier) type, since providing explicitly it to SSHRI doesn't do lower casing:\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import *; print(SSHRI(\"ssh://username@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git\").hostname)' \nC-Alias\n```\nso IMHO clearly something to be fixed since I think behavior should be more consistent there in \"guessing\".\n\nAnd if I get it right from the failure you are observing -- ssh is \"case sensitive\" whenever it comes to aliases, but when it comes to DNS provided hostnames, that is where the case does not matter and it uses lower-cased version.\n\nSo overall I think we should indeed fix it up for `RI()` guessing to not lower case.  What do you think @datalad/developers?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1547610863,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE3MjI2MTUyNQ=="},"target":"7a95d5f6031827ff015f5c17867963861a01fd3e86aa6f314e935b81dc835118","message":"\u003e #### Have you had any success using DataLad before? (to assess your expertise/prior luck. We would welcome your testimonial additions to https://github.com/datalad/datalad/wiki/Testimonials as well)\n\u003e \n\u003e datalad is great!\n\nthanks - really glad to hear\n\nI feel that somewhere we or python library we use forces hostnames to lower case since generally hostnames should be not case sensitive, see e.g. https://serverfault.com/questions/261341/is-the-hostname-case-sensitive for additional pointers:\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import RI; print(RI(\"ssh://username@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git\").hostname)'\nc-alias\n```\nbut interestingly it is a \"side-effect\" of guessing the RI (resource identifier) type, since providing explicitly it to SSHRI doesn't do lower casing:\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import *; print(SSHRI(\"ssh://username@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git\").hostname)' \nC-Alias\n```\nso IMHO clearly something to be fixed since I think behavior should be more consistent there in \"guessing\".\n\nAnd if I get it right from the failure you are observing -- ssh is \"case sensitive\" whenever it comes to aliases, but when it comes to DNS provided hostnames, that is where the case does not matter and it uses lower-cased version.\n\nSo overall I think we should indeed fix it up for `RI()` guessing to not lower case.  What do you think @datalad/developers?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1547610891,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE3MjI2MTY2Mg=="},"target":"7a95d5f6031827ff015f5c17867963861a01fd3e86aa6f314e935b81dc835118","message":"\u003e #### Have you had any success using DataLad before? (to assess your expertise/prior luck. We would welcome your testimonial additions to https://github.com/datalad/datalad/wiki/Testimonials as well)\n\u003e \n\u003e datalad is great!\n\nthanks - really glad to hear\n\nI feel that somewhere we or python force hostnames to lower case since generally hostnames should be not case sensitive, see e.g. https://serverfault.com/questions/261341/is-the-hostname-case-sensitive for additional pointers:\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import RI; print(RI(\"ssh://username@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git\").hostname)'\nc-alias\n```\nbut interestingly it is a \"side-effect\" of guessing the RI (resource identifier) type, since providing explicitly it to SSHRI doesn't do lower casing:\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import *; print(SSHRI(\"ssh://username@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git\").hostname)' \nC-Alias\n```\nso IMHO clearly something to be fixed since I think behavior should be more consistent there in \"guessing\".\n\nAnd if I get it right from the failure you are observing -- ssh is \"case sensitive\" whenever it comes to aliases, but when it comes to DNS provided hostnames, that is where the case does not matter and it uses lower-cased version.\n\nSo overall I think we should indeed fix it up for `RI()` guessing to not lower case.  What do you think @datalad/developers?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1547611761,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NDY0NTExNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3123#issuecomment-454645114"},"message":"one additional bit of information -- I was incorrectly assuming it is an SSHRI but we autodetect it as a regular URL\n```\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import *; print(type(RI(\"ssh://username@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git\")))'\n\u003cclass 'datalad.support.network.URL'\u003e\n```\nsince it does conform a regular URL -- protocol://user:password@host[:port]/path, although a slight gotcha there in a `[:port]` portion, that `port` is empty.  Not yet sure if that it is strictly required...  And then hostname gets lower cased, which is probably as it should be.  But `ssh` itself does treat those alises case sensitive even in case of using `ssh://...` form.  Not sure yet if that is entirely kosher and not more ad-hoc ;)\n\nAnyways, if you want to force a regular SSHRI which would retain your casing, try using SSH (or our SSHRI) way to define your target, i.e. strip `ssh://` prefix:\n```\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import *; print(type(RI(\"username@C-Alias:/home/username/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git\")))'         \n\u003cclass 'datalad.support.network.SSHRI'\u003e\n```\ndoes that work?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"6f5e24e1b2268edeef4cca4da912a38f754be7a8"},"timestamp":1548348438,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NzI2NDgzNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3123#issuecomment-457264836"},"message":"Hi Yarick,\n\nThanks for the comprehensive reply! I tried updating the git remote and gitsubmodule definitions for the dataset without an `ssh://` prefix and it didnt work either :( \n\nIt seems that datalad is still reading the original url .. not sure where else to update it\n\n```\n[Level 5] Detected url ri\n[DEBUG  ] Parsed version of URL 'ssh://admin@c-alias:/Users/admin/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git%3A22' differs from original 'ssh://admin@C-Alias:/Users/admin/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git'\n.... more messages ommitted ...\n[DEBUG  ] No files found needing fetching.\n```","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"6f5e24e1b2268edeef4cca4da912a38f754be7a8"},"timestamp":1548348438,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE3NTg4NjQwMg=="},"target":"9c397c0c7d0dee6655f1d66e8f30c7b26454794fde88044a0035879daf9aa8bc","message":"Hi Yarick,\n\nThanks for the comprehensive reply! I tried updating the git remote and gitsubmodule definitions for the dataset without an `ssh://` prefix and it didnt work either :( \n\nIt seems that datalad is still reading the original url .. not sure where else to update it\n\n```\n[Level 5] Detected url ri\n[DEBUG  ] Parsed version of URL 'ssh://admin@c-alias/Users/admin/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git%3A22' differs from original 'ssh://admin@C-Alias:/Users/admin/datasets/central-repository/dataset.git'\n.... more messages ommitted ...\n[DEBUG  ] No files found needing fetching.\n```\nEDIT::\nActually,  I just noticed that the parsed version doesn't include the ':' between path and URL.. why would that be??","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1548388701,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NzQ0NzUzMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3123#issuecomment-457447531"},"message":"\u003e Actually, I just noticed that the parsed version doesn't include the ':' between path and URL.. why would that be??\n\nbecause there is no port, and thus no need for `:/` -- it is a URL so `protocol://host[:port]/path`\n\n\n\n\u003e Thanks for the comprehensive reply! I tried updating the git remote and gitsubmodule definitions for the dataset without an `ssh://` prefix and it didnt work either :(\n\nand submodule sections in `.git/config`?  I would just  `grep -r ssh://admin@c-alias .` and find the culprit","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"6f5e24e1b2268edeef4cca4da912a38f754be7a8"},"timestamp":1548545198,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1Nzg3NDk3MQ==","github-url":"h